          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-80fb00222eee79c5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Christian’s direct “Je t’aime tant” (“I love you so much”) is principally an avowal presenting his love for Roxane as true. It neither undertakes a distinct commitment nor functions as a reflex-like affective discharge: its repetition results from his inability to elaborate on the avowal as Roxane requests.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Christian directly addresses Roxane with the full utterance “Je t’aime tant”; the highlighted words are its core clause.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Christian has rejected Cyrano’s assistance and is trying to speak for himself. His avowal appears sincere but rhetorically impoverished: Roxane accepts love as the established “theme” and asks him to explain how he loves her, while Christian can only intensify and repeat the basic assertion. The comic failure concerns eloquence and courtship performance, not the semantic force of the love claim.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“CHRISTIAN: Je t’aime tant.”\",\"supports\":\"The declarative first-person wording, intensified by “tant,” strongly supports truth-conditional avowal.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Christian says he is tired of borrowing letters and speeches and will now “parler moi-même” (speak for himself).\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is Christian’s own direct avowal rather than words currently supplied or revoiced from Cyrano.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane responds, “Sans doute ! Et puis ?” and asks him to explain how he loves her; Christian later manages only “Mais… beaucoup.”\",\"supports\":\"The scene treats the love proposition as understood but inadequately elaborated, favoring T rather than P, E, or O.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The surrounding sequence repeats “Je vous aime,” “Je t’aime,” and “Je t’aime tant” after Roxane instructs Christian to “Brodez.”\",\"supports\":\"The repetition is a deliberate, if inarticulate, attempt to state and amplify his feelings, not positive evidence of reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. Christian’s sincerity, desire, rhetorical incompetence, and the comic mismatch with Roxane’s expectations are contextual features and create no residual pressure beyond T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The temporal/intensifying modifier “tant” strengthens degree of love but does not alter the utterance’s basic force.\"}"
        }
      ],
